Cover memo

## Why you are late

To

Field team, new assignment

From

N. Garrity, liaison analyst, Regional Analysis Office (Department liaison)

Re

Teague County, T. Ruddick, audio recording, Parcel 0-114

Date

Saturday 31 October

You are being deployed late, and you should know why before you read anything else.

Five days ago a 24-year-old woman named Tessa Ruddick went on a date, started a personal-safety app on her phone and walked into a closed parcel of county land. The app recorded 71 minutes of audio and uploaded it to her account. Her family gave the login to the sheriff's office on Wednesday. On Thursday the county and the regional federal office reviewed the recording. On Friday morning they closed the file as a psychiatric episode with presumed self-harm. I had no part in that.

On Friday afternoon a state audio examiner, Marguerite Soto, refused to sign off on the review. She objected to the first reviewers excluding about two minutes of audio as non-speech artifact. That evening an archivist checking for similar cases cross-referenced a 2003 cold case, the disappearance of Elizabeth Marsh. The 2003 file has carried a passive Department watch since 2004. The match fired at 18:02. I was brought in at 18:40, listened to the whole recording that night and recommended a full response. Operations agreed on Saturday morning. That is a day and a half we did not have.

**What I think, and what I cannot support.** I think the recording is what it sounds like. I cannot show that. The county's reading is just as unsupported as mine, with one exception: the specificity. Her descriptions are detailed, in a consistent order, and several are confirmed by a 2003 file that nobody on the new case had read. A delusion that agrees with twenty-year-old evidence is not behaving like a delusion.

**What we do not know.** What is in Parcel 0-114. Whether Tessa is alive in any sense you would recognize. Who fired the shot. What she means by "the way". Our own records are close to empty: three earlier mentions of similar tin-plate posts (see Archives), none with an outcome.

**What I am asking.** Locate and assess. Talk to people before you go near the parcel. Do not cross the cordon without a filed Recon Report; I cannot clear you without one, and the county will not clear you at all. Your cover is a state health department environmental survey, and the credentials are in your kit. All desks are open. Operations has staged support and has not told me the schedule.

The excluded segments are restored in the recording below. If you listen to them, turn the volume down first.

N. Garrity

