## Elizabeth Marsh, 2003

Teague County cold case 03-0217Inactive since 2005

Elizabeth Marsh, 19, was home from her first year of college and worked evenings at a diner on Route 41. She left her shift at about 22:00 on Friday 20 June 2003. Her car was found on Sunday 22 June at the gravel pullout off County Road 12. The driver's door was unlocked, the keys were in the ignition, the dome light was on and the battery was dead. The dashboard was covered in dead flies, species not recorded. The responding deputy's first report says the hood was warm. That line was struck from the final log. No body was found and nobody was charged.

Several local young men, known around town collectively as "The Boys," were interviewed. Nine names are on file with the county and are not attached here. Chat logs recovered in July 2003 from a shared computer in a family garage show members of the group watching Elizabeth's shifts and planning to bring her to a place they call "the treehouse." Nothing else was established. The logs have a ten-day gap beginning on the day she vanished.

